{"id":"who-handled-it","title":"Who handled it: outcomes by case planner","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"Do like cases end differently depending on which Planning Department case planner was assigned?","finding":"Among 35 case planners with 60 or more decided applications since 2015, the share not approved runs from 7.5% to 50.0%; 2 of them sit further from what the mix of cases predicts than chance would explain, and in those cases every failed application was a withdrawal or cancellation, not a refusal.","how":"The “assigned to planner” field of each decided application. “Expected” applies the citywide rate for the same approval type, kind of proposal and period to each of the planner’s cases. “Beyond chance” uses a strict line because many planners are compared at once. Team and intake mailboxes are left out. Sorted by the size of the gap.","limits":"The case planner does not decide: the Planning Commission, the Zoning Administrator or the Historic Preservation Commission does, and most failed applications are withdrawn by the applicant. Planners are assigned by area and by type of work, so a planner’s figure largely repeats the district’s; some are given the hardest files, and a senior planner who closes out stale files will show many withdrawals and cancellations. The field shows the last planner assigned, not everyone who worked on the case. Assignment is not misconduct. The hearing minutes, roll-call votes and appeal decisions now being collected would show who voted, who objected and what reason was recorded in each case.","columns":[{"k":"planner","t":"Case planner (as recorded)","type":"person"},{"k":"decided","t":"Applications decided","type":"int"},{"k":"notApproved","t":"Not approved","type":"pct"},{"k":"expected","t":"Expected for the same kinds","type":"pct"},{"k":"gap","t":"Gap","type":"pct"},{"k":"beyondChance","t":"Beyond chance?","type":"text"},{"k":"withdrawn","t":"Withdrawn or cancelled","type":"pct"},{"k":"denied","t":"Refused outright","type":"p